        "title": "Systematics and Major Groups",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Avian systematics underwent a revolution from 1987 (Sibley and Ahlquist DNA hybridization) through 2014-2015 (whole-genome phylogenomics), overturning many morphology-based groupings. The current consensus is the Jarvis (2014) and Prum (2015) phylogenomic trees, though some internal Neoaves topology remains contested. Modern checklist species concepts (IOC, Clements) differ in how many species to recognize."
    },
